; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED for this corpus, after nvidia-isaac/Isaac-cap's `gear_insertion` --
; three NVIDIA Factory gears placed on a plate and scored SEATED AND STILL, at
; three post offsets stated as constants. The medium tier here is the same three
; stations, and two things about it are different.
;
; THE BOARD IS ASSEMBLABLE BY CONSTRUCTION. Two external involute gears of one
; module mesh when, and only when, their centres are module * (z_a + z_b) / 2
; apart. So the pegs are placed BY that rule rather than at measured offsets:
; `gearing.chain_centres` computes them from the tooth counts, and there is no
; way to author a board whose gears do not mesh. The check is live, not a
; comment -- `mesh_phase` sweeps a pitch looking for a clocking that clears, and
; refuses a pair whose centre distance is not theirs. Moved 2 mm together, a
; 16- and a 20-tooth gear have no clocking that clears at all.
;
; AND THE SCORE IS WHETHER IT WORKS. A motor pinion the policy never touches
; drives the first station; the button starts it; success is every gear turning
; at its own ratio. That is not a restatement of "seated". A gear on the wrong
; peg, or a gear seated and then knocked, breaks the chain AT that station and
; everything downstream of it stops -- which is exactly the property that makes
; the whole train worth measuring instead of three placements counted up.
;
; WHAT MAKES IT HARD. The bore has 0.3 mm of radial clearance on its peg, and
; the peg stands 8 mm proud of a seated gear so the bore is captured before the
; teeth are anywhere near their neighbour's. That ordering is the task's own
; lead-in: a gear that is on its peg is free to turn, and turning is how it
; finds the tooth space. Meeting a neighbour tooth-corner to tooth-corner
; instead is a jam, which is why every tooth carries a 0.6 mm chamfer on both
; faces.
;
; WHAT THE NUMBERS BELOW ARE. Measured on the assembled board by
; tasks/gear_mesh/rigs/gear_bench.py, three gears at a commanded 4 rad/s: every gear holds
; 0.972 of its ideal ratio, and 0.972 is exactly the pinion's own servo droop
; of 3.89/4.00 -- the mesh loses nothing at all. The 5th percentile of that
; reading is 0.94. A gear whose chain is broken upstream is driven by nothing
; and reads 0. Nothing lands in between, which is why the gate sits at 0.3.
;
; A RATE HERE IS NET ANGLE OVER HALF A SECOND, not a sampled angular velocity,
; and that is not a smoothing convenience. A driven gear rattles through its own
; backlash: sampled instantaneously, the same healthy chain's 5th percentile is
; 0.35, 0.14 and 0.05 of ideal going down the stations, so a gate placed
; anywhere useful is crossed downward several times a second and every hold-for
; behind it restarts forever. The board keeps the window because a measure that
; remembers cannot be an atom -- the mint gate requires purity -- which is the
; same reason polish's spindle publishes `disc_speed` rather than minting one.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem MESH_THE_GEAR_TRAIN)

  (:suite gear_mesh)

  (:language put each gear on its own peg so they mesh, then press the red
             button at the near end of the board to start the train)

  ; The cell, the arm, the physics, the cameras and the episode count are the
  ; suite's -- gear_mesh.suite, beside this file. What is this tier's alone is
  ; its step budget and where its gears start.
  (:staging
    ; Three picks, three insertions and a press, with the settle and the hold.
    ; Generous on purpose: the budget is sized for the retries a gear that lands
    ; tooth-on-tooth needs, not for a run that never does.
    (:max-steps 9000)
    ; Where each named gear starts: all three gears flat on the mat.
    ; DERIVED, not measured -- written from assets/layout.py's arithmetic
    ; (`write_start_poses`) and pinned to it by tests/test_gear_mesh.py. A
    ; disc lying flat has its centre exactly half a face above what it lies
    ; on, so a spawn pose is a settled pose by construction. Recorded per
    ; gear because the gear records cannot carry it: a gear_wheel_20t is one
    ; thing wherever it lies, and which spot -- or which peg -- is this
    ; tier's.
    (:start-poses poses/train.json)
  )

  (:objects
    gear_a - gear_wheel_16t
    gear_b - gear_wheel_20t
    gear_c - gear_wheel_24t
  )

  (:fixtures
    mat   - gear_mat
    board - gear_board_train
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; The world an episode starts in, re-verified after settling at every reset.
  ;
  ; Both clauses are geometric and read off body poses, which are settled and
  ; correct at the reset instant. Two candidate clauses were NOT written for
  ; polish's recorded reason: a spin reads zero at t=0 whatever the scene is,
  ; because the environment zeroes joint velocities after entity resets, and a
  ; contact force reads zero because the force atom's window is empty at
  ; verification. An init that warns on every clean episode teaches people to
  ; ignore the warning.
  (:init
    (loose board)
    (unstarted board)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; ASSEMBLE, THEN START, THEN TURN -- and in that order, which is what the
  ; nested `after`s say. `after` is binary, so a three-stage sequence nests;
  ; each event latches on its rising edge, so a gear briefly lifted after the
  ; train is running does not un-start it.
  ;
  ; The order matters and is not decoration. Pressing the button first and
  ; assembling into a running train is a different task -- harder, and one this
  ; board's torque ceiling would stall rather than reward.
  ;
  ; `worst_of`, not a flat `and`: success is still every child, but the reward
  ; becomes the WEAKEST station's rather than the fraction of stations passing,
  ; and `metrics["worst"]` names which gear stalled. A flat conjunction would
  ; also pay a do-nothing policy for whichever clauses a settled scene already
  ; satisfies.
  ;
  ; Per-station TRIPLES rather than loose clauses, for the grocery lesson the
  ; goal lint records: clauses grouped by the thing they are about read as "this
  ; gear is on its peg, turning, and let go of", and ungrouped they read as a
  ; score. The third one is not ceremony -- a gear still in the jaws while the
  ; train runs is being held in mesh by the robot, and polish and box_decant both
  ; end on the same requirement for the same reason. It is also what makes the
  ; goal name the gears at all: everything else here is said about the board,
  ; because a published state is the board's to publish and an ObjectSpec cannot
  ; publish one.
  (:goal
    (once
      (after (>= (gears-seated board) 3)
        (after (started board)
          (hold-for 1.0
            (worst_of
              (And (gear0-on-peg board) (gear0-turning board) (not (gripped gear_a.body)))
              (And (gear1-on-peg board) (gear1-turning board) (not (gripped gear_b.body)))
              (And (gear2-on-peg board) (gear2-turning board) (not (gripped gear_c.body)))
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; The share of stations both seated and turning, gated at all of them.
  ; Deliberately not `(fraction)`, which would pay for the settled conjuncts a
  ; scene satisfies before the policy moves. Read the do-nothing floor before
  ; believing any of it.
  ;
  ; The goal lint notes that no goal clause measures `turning-fraction`, and the
  ; note is right and the choice is deliberate. The two say the same thing in the
  ; two shapes each is good at: the goal is `worst_of` over stations because a
  ; verdict should name the station that stalled, and a reward is a scalar that
  ; has to rise smoothly as stations come good, which the worst of them does not.
  ; A chain that gains its third working gear moves the fraction and not the
  ; worst.
  (:reward (progress (turning-fraction board) (:gate 1.0)))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing. `gear*-ratio` is what makes a trace
  ; legible -- "station 2 at 0.97 and station 3 at 0.00" is a chain with a hole
  ; in it, and reads nothing like a train that never started. `gear*-signed`
  ; carries the alternation: external gears counter-rotate, so a chain whose
  ; signs do not alternate is not a chain.
  (:observe
    (turning-fraction board)
    (gears-seated board)
    (press-travel board)
    (pinion-speed board)
    (gear0-ratio board)
    (gear1-ratio board)
    (gear2-ratio board)
    (gear0-signed board)
    (gear1-signed board)
    (gear2-signed board)
    (speed gear_a)
    (speed gear_b)
    (speed gear_c)
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier original)
    (:source "Authored for this corpus. The task shape -- gears onto an assembly
              board -- is nvidia-isaac/Isaac-cap's `gear_insertion`, which is
              itself the NIST Assembly Task Board's gear station by way of Isaac
              Lab's Factory assets. Nothing is vendored: the profiles are
              generated involutes at module 2.5 mm, 20 degree pressure angle,
              1.25 dedendum, 0.15 mm backlash. The two spur gears already in
              this repository under assets/vab_tool_sort are a module-5 pair at
              110 and 160 mm outside diameter, wider than any gripper here and
              of unestablished licence; they are a cross-check on the generator,
              not its source.")
    (:note "Each gear is one rigid body carrying one convex prism per tooth plus
            a ring of hub sectors -- 40 parts for a 16-tooth gear -- because
            MuJoCo collides a mesh as its convex hull and a gear imported whole
            is a disc with neither teeth nor bore. Every part is convex BY
            CONSTRUCTION rather than by decomposition: an external involute
            flank is convex, and gear_parts asserts that against the profile it
            just built rather than taking it from a textbook. The pinion is a
            velocity-driven revolute on polish's rotary-spindle pattern, gated
            from inside the captured graph by a latch the button sets; the
            button is this repository's first prismatic joint. CAL-tagged
            thresholds are re-measured against the shipped graph before
            sign-off.")
  )
)
